---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
---
<Site
  title="WhiteThroated"
  description="WhiteThroated is an experimental high-performance web framework written entirely in Swift. Pre-alpha."
>
  <section class="home-hero">
  <h1>WhiteThroated</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    WhiteThroated is an experimental high-performance web framework written entirely in <span class="swift-name"><img src="https://cdn.simpleicons.org/swift/F05138" alt="" />Swift</span>.
    It is pre-alpha: API compatibility is intentionally secondary to establishing a fast,
    measurable runtime architecture. Expect breaking changes before 1.0 and pin exact releases.
  </p>
  </section>

  <div class="accordion-group">
    <details class="accordion" name="landing-sections">
      <summary><h2>Two non-negotiable goals</h2></summary>
      <div class="accordion-panel">
        <ol>
          <li>Express.js-level ergonomics for application developers.</li>
          <li>
            Runtime overhead low enough to compete with the fastest practical server frameworks, with
            Actix Web as an external performance reference and raw SwiftNIO as the lower-bound control.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="accordion" name="landing-sections">
      <summary><h2>How it is built</h2></summary>
      <div class="accordion-panel">
        <p>
          Application code uses the <code>WhiteThroated</code> module (routes, middleware,
          Request/Response). Execution runs on <code>WhiteThroatedRuntime</code>, an internal module on
          SwiftNIO that owns the HTTP/1.1 pipeline, dispatch state machine, and response writer.
          Configuration is mutable during setup and frozen once serving begins. Four responder paths exist
          so cheap routes never pay for features they don't use: prepared immutable responses, synchronous
          handlers, event-loop-native futures, and opt-in async/await. The default transport is Tier 1
          HTTP/1 with a stock SwiftNIO fallback for request-streaming apps.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="accordion" name="landing-sections">
      <summary><h2>The target is overhead, not a number</h2></summary>
      <div class="accordion-panel">
        <p>
          The target is not a requests-per-second number. The target is to minimize framework overhead
          until Swift runtime, SwiftNIO, kernel, and hardware costs dominate. The honest comparison order
          is: same WhiteThroated path before/after, then equivalent raw SwiftNIO, then responder modes,
          then external frameworks only after Swift-side overhead is understood.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="accordion" name="landing-sections">
      <summary><h2>What WhiteThroated offers</h2></summary>
      <div class="accordion-panel">
        <ul>
          <li>Express-inspired routing with synchronous, future, and async handler styles.</li>
          <li>Prepared responses that precompute immutable content at setup.</li>
          <li>Benchmarks against raw SwiftNIO and external controls, with full methodology published.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          What it does not offer: superlatives without a cited, reproducible comparison. No
          best-case single-metric configuration is ever presented as winning every metric.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="accordion" name="landing-sections">
      <summary><h2>Benchmarks with provenance</h2></summary>
      <div class="accordion-panel">
        <p>
          Every published figure carries its framework commit, toolchain, OS and CPU, load-generator
          identity, measurement date, gate status, and a link to archived raw data. Start with the
          <a href="/results">results matrix</a>, read <a href="/methodology">how benchmarks are run and judged</a>,
          or browse the <a href="/reports">report archive</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</Site>
